@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Design-tokens-Typography-reference a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Design tokens: Typography (reference)";
    ds:summary "This document describes the Pragma typography tokens: all tokens that are needed in relation to text and typography. For the most part these tokens will be of the “typography” type. But some tokens like fontSize, lineHeight and baseline nudges might fall under different categories.  \n\nFor general Design tokens information go to [Design tokens: Overview](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-GnfOU3MM-3/_rui-BlNN-e8ieQ)";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "## What does the typography token category cover?\n\nIn the current version of the W3C design token specification the following values are required (as in not optional) for a typography composite token and does not allow for other values:\n\n- fontFamily\n- fontSize\n- fontWeight\n- letterSpacing\n- lineHeight\n\n### **Unsupported typography types**\n\nSince this version of the specification has been released there have been discussions in the [feedback thread](https://github.com/design-tokens/community-group/issues/102) on whether to support inheritance (not making the 5 values above mandatory) and supporting more than the 5 mentioned properties (things like font styling etc.) Even though the specification does not support it at least for some platforms we will need certain additional properties other than the ones that are currently supported. In order to stay compliant with the specification we move these additionally required properties to the “$extensions” property. As part of a “com.canonical.typography” extensions package. From there we can build a plugin for our build process that is able to parse these extensions and output the properties that we need. \n\nThe “com.canonical.typography” extensions are set up in a way that it will not interfere with non extension tokens. Meaning if a system consuming the tokens does not support the extension it will still output valid tokens. The additional data of the extension will just be missing.\n\nAs soon as these properties are officially supported we can move them out of the extensions and into the officially supported version.\n\n### **Tier specific needs**\n\nDifferent tiers have different needs so we will be creating different sets of typographic styles for each of the tiers. Some values like font size and line height are dimension tokens, so they will be part of the dimension category. We will still define them here as part of this specification because they are strongly correlated to the typographic design tokens they are used with.\n\n## Tokens\n\n### **Dimension scale**\n\nIn order to be able to do the root font size scaling that we are doing on the xLarge breakpoint we need an additional primitive token we can use in the semantic rootFontSize token.\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 86\n\n​\n\n### **Root font size**\n\nThis token is needed to control the root font size scaling at larger breakpoints.\n\n#### Semantic\n\n**Small breakpoint**\n\nTable 87\n\n**Medium breakpoint**\n\nTable 88\n\n**Large breakpoint**\n\nTable 89\n\n**xLarge breakpoint**\n\nTable 90\n\n​\n\n### **Baseline grid**\n\nThe spacing in our design system is based on a [baseline](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baseline_(typography)) grid. What that means in short is that there are imaginary horizontal grid lines that go from the top of the page down. The baseline height determines the distance between those imaginary lines. And in this system the baseline of every text is supposed to sit on one of those imaginary lines.\n\n#### Semantic\n\n**Small breakpoint**\n\nTable 91\n\n**Medium breakpoint**\n\nTable 92\n\n**Large breakpoint**\n\nTable 93\n\n**xLarge breakpoint**\n\nTable 94\n\n​\n\n### **Font size**\n\nThis is the “palette” of font sizes that are allowed to be used across our digital platforms. The scale is generated based on an algorithm (f_i = f_0 × r^(i/n)) from an article by Spencer Mortensen which can be read [here](https://spencermortensen.com/articles/typographic-scale/). The code used to generate the scale is [attached in a separate document tab](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HXYvbs9I_EJm2cCwjiNm_DTSwTT3IHQf6xl7mIpX2AY/edit?tab=t.sd056pym3ojg#heading=h.5gtq92t2hz3m).\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 95\n\n​\n\n### **Line height**\n\nLine heights can be quite tricky to pick. So for now we have gone with manually picking the line heights below. We chose line heights based on two requirements:\n\n​\n\n1. To roughly fit line height/ type size ratios on a scale starting from 1.5 (a comfortable line height ratio for paragraphs) to 1.1,  following typography best practices of reducing line heights as the type size increases, and the number of lines decreases. \n2. We aim to map line heights to a 8px baseline grid, and use halves (4px) only when strongly needed.\n\nWe might look into algorithmically picking line heights in the future. The line heights are defined as numbers because that is what the w3c specification calls for. If in the future the specification also allows for dimension tokens for line heights we can move the line height tokens to be of type dimension.\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 96\n\n​\n\n### **Baseline nudges**\n\nAs outlined [above](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HXYvbs9I_EJm2cCwjiNm_DTSwTT3IHQf6xl7mIpX2AY/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.4snikwf9rvhm) we are using a baseline system. Most digital platforms we want to use our design system on do not natively support snapping the baseline of a text to the baseline grid. As such we need to manually add spacing on top of a text element to make sure the baseline of the text is nudged onto the baseline grid. And we need to add spacing after the text element so the text element's height is a multiple of the baseline height (so the next element after it can start from a known baseline grid position).\n\n​\n\nDifferent platforms we develop for (Chromium, Firefox, Webkit, Figma, Flutter) all have slightly different ways of rendering their fonts and therefore where the baseline of the font is rendered varies (different positioning and rounding strategies). So it does not seem possible to have a singular set of spacing values that work perfectly for every platform. So what we should practically aim for is using a set of spacing values that has the lowest discrepancy for the most number of users across our platforms. \n\n​\n\nWe have tried a *mathematically correct* approach of calculating the base line position and the needed spacing from font metrics but because of the mentioned positioning and rounding strategies by the platforms mentioned above those *mathematically correct* values are very often not *empirically correct*. The platforms render it slightly differently.\n\n​\n\nThat's why for the values below we went with the approach of automatically measuring the baseline position in the platform most of our users will consume our design system in (Chromium on a 1dppx screen). This achieved the most accurate result we have had so far on most platforms. Meaning it is spot on for most sizes on most platforms. Sometimes by 0.5px and at most by 1px (only observed for four sizes on Figma).\n\n​\n\nThe automatic measurement code and a simple html page to test the values on different platforms is attached in [a separate document tab](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HXYvbs9I_EJm2cCwjiNm_DTSwTT3IHQf6xl7mIpX2AY/edit?tab=t.4wjaufrr27s6).\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 97\n\n​\n\n### **Font family**\n\nThe main font we want to use is Ubuntu Sans. If the font, for whatever reason, is not available, then we need fallback fonts. As fallback fonts we want to have whatever is the default font of the system the user is using. For CSS there is a keyword for it: “system-ui”. This keyword unfortunately seems to be flawed as it is selecting a font in asian languages that is not ideal. See [W3C CSS discussion on github](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/3658) for context.\n\nSo because of that we still need “old school” system font stacks. Below is a list of the operating systems / desktop environments and their system font (as well as emoji font according to [this wikipedia page](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Implementation_of_emoji)).\n\n​\n\n**Sans system font stack:**\n\nTable 98\n\n​\n\n**Mono system font stack:**\n\nTable 99\n\n​\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 100\n\n#### Semantic\n\nTable 111\n\n​\n\n### **Font weight**\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 112\n\n​\n\n### **Font style**\n\n#### Primitives\n\nTable 113\n\n​  \n\n\n### **Font position**\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 114\n\n​\n\n### **Figure style**\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 115\n\n​\n\n### **Text decoration**\n\n#### Primitive\n\nTable 137\n\n​\n\n### **Letter case**\n\n#### Primitives\n\nTable 138\n\n​  \n\n\n### **Letter spacing**\n\n#### Primitives\n\nTable 139\n\n​\n\n## Composite tokens\n\nCertain components apply heading styles to non-heading text elements. This approach reflects design workflows where visual hierarchy is conceptualized in terms of existing heading levels rather than specific typographic values. By referencing heading styles, these components maintain visual consistency and automatically inherit any future modifications to the base heading definitions. This method is used for text elements that require emphasis or visual prominence within the interface.\n\nText that is currently x-small in Vanilla (Badge) will need to use text.primary.bold. \n\n“text.primary.$root” is meant to be used as the default text style for UI elements. “text.primary.prose.$root” is meant to be used for flowing text. It uses a style of numbers that improves readability of numbers in flowing prose text.\n\n### **Global typography set**\n\n#### Semantic\n\nTable 140\n\n​\n\n**Primary**\n\nTable 141\n\n​\n\n**Secondary**\n\nTable 142\n\n​\n\n**Tertiary**\n\nTable 143\n\n​\n\n**Headings**\n\nTable 144\n\n​\n\n### **Sites set**\n\nTable 145\n\n​\n\n### **Documentation set**\n\n#### Semantic\n\n**Primary**\n\nTable 146\n\n​\n\n**Secondary**\n\nTable 147\n\n​\n\n**Tertiary**\n\nTable 148\n\n​\n\n**Headings**\n\nTable 149\n\n​\n\n### **Application set**\n\n#### Semantic\n\n**Primary**\n\nTable 150\n\n​\n\n**Secondary**\n\nTable 151\n\n​\n\n**Tertiary**\n\nTable 152\n\n​\n\n**Headings**\n\nTable 153";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Reference.
